plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:5:Evidence convention: **[verified: URL]** identifies external documentation; **[local: path]** identifies repository evidence I read. **[assumption]** identifies a proposal, engineering judgment, target, or unresolved dependency, rather than measured behavior. Unless explicitly attributed otherwise, implementation choices below are **[assumption]**. No resource figures are benchmarks.
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:7:## 1. Decision: Tauri 2, React, TypeScript, Rust, a small Swift capture helper
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:11:Tauri's core manages windows and tray interfaces; its web content uses operating-system webviews, specifically WKWebView on macOS and WebView2 on Windows. It does not bundle those webview libraries. That establishes the structural opportunity to keep a small native resident core and destroy unused interface windows; it does **not** prove a particular RAM saving. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/concept/process-model/] [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/reference/webview-versions/]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:32:Electron is the close second, and its advantage is substantial: its documented main/renderer model supplies a known Chromium-based environment, with a renderer for each BrowserWindow. For a terminal-heavy IDE, I would accept its baseline cost. Macroscope spends most hours observing with no visible editor. Choosing Electron to improve the minority editing workload would make its permanent runtime a product trade-off; I choose Tauri instead. No claim that every Tauri app uses less memory is warranted. [verified: https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/process-model] **[assumption: product weighting]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:34:For generated content, Electron recommends alternatives such as WebContentsView rather than its webview tag, and its security guide cautions about untrusted content and privileged APIs. Electron would still need a deliberately constrained bridge and execution policy; switching frameworks does not solve generated-code security. [verified: https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/api/webview-tag] [verified: https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/security]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:36:Flutter is credible because the owner knows it and it supports Windows/macOS desktop. flutter_rust_bridge provides the Dart/Rust binding route. However, the official webview_flutter package currently lists Android, iOS, and macOS, leaving Windows web hosting to a different integration. A Flutter shell plus Rust plus native capture plus web-hosted tools creates more boundaries than this product needs. Reject it for this app, not for desktop generally. [verified: https://docs.flutter.dev/platform-integration/desktop] [verified: https://github.com/fzyzcjy/flutter_rust_bridge] [verified: https://pub.dev/packages/webview_flutter] **[assumption: complexity assessment]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:40:## 2. User-visible architecture and performance contract
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:44:Tauri documents native tray construction and menu callbacks in Rust; implement those there so no webview is necessary for Pause or Quit. Create Dashboard, Builder, and each pinned tool as distinct logical windows. Keep ordinary title bars and standard keyboard shortcuts initially; a borderless imitation of native chrome buys little. Destroy the Dashboard/Builder webviews after closure, retaining compact navigation state in Rust. Test this lifecycle because operating-system webview resource release is not a contractual memory guarantee. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/learn/system-tray/] **[assumption: lifecycle]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:50:## 3. Component and process model
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:86:3. **Model worker.** A short-lived Rust executable consumes sanitized workflow bundles, batches pattern classification, handles API requests/cost accounting, and owns Muse supervision during builds. It cannot query the entire capture archive: the core supplies a job-specific bundle. Worker restart must not restart capture. No local inference server is required: the brief mandates billed API-key model use. **[assumption]** [local: plan/BRIEF.md]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:87:4. **Muse host.** One `muse serve` child per active build workspace and immutable sandbox posture, not a global unrestricted host. Reuse within that build only; stop after an idle timeout. MSP offers session operations and approvals, while serve's sandbox posture is fixed at host construction. Keep analysis-only jobs separate from shell-enabled build jobs. [local: plan/reference/muse-cli-help.txt; plan/reference/msp/msp.d.ts] **[assumption: host policy]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:90:On macOS use a small Swift package with ScreenCaptureKit, AppKit/workspace notifications, Accessibility calls, and on-device redaction. ScreenCaptureKit is Apple's capture framework; AXIsProcessTrustedWithOptions checks whether the calling process is a trusted accessibility client. Prefer Swift over objc2 here because native samples and debugging are more direct; keep the transport boundary language-neutral. [verified: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/screencapturekit] [verified: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/applicationservices/1459186-axisprocesstrustedwithoptions] **[assumption: implementation choice]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:92:A major early spike must establish which signed app/helper identity macOS attributes permissions to, and verify grant/revoke, update, relaunch, and login behavior with the packaged app. Do not assume the dashboard's grant transfers to its helper. On Windows use Windows.Graphics.Capture with user-selected capture plus a UI Automation adapter. Microsoft documents system selection UI and visible capture indication; preserve them rather than promising invisible parity with Mac. [verified: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/media-authoring-processing/screen-capture] **[assumption: acceptance gate]**
