/** A request id (SS1.3): client-chosen string or integer. `1` and `"1"` do not compare equal; each direction owns its own id space. */
export type RequestId = number | string;

/** The SS5.3.3 presentation receipt — the client's response to a server-initiated request (`approval/request`, `userInput/request`).  The response acknowledges presentation only ("a surface showed or will show this"): it changes no state, the server uses it for diagnostics alone, and the decision/answer travels as a command (`approval/decide`, `userInput/answer`). An error response or a dropped connection means this connection could not present the request; the approval or prompt stays pending and the request is re-issued on the next subscribe (SS5.6). There is no dismiss-without-deciding on the wire. */
export interface RequestReceipt {
}

/** A `turn/retracted` settlement outcome (tdd SS4.5.1, ADR 50074 D1). Open enum (D-033): decode is tolerant — a value outside the list reads as `Accepted` (the event's historical meaning — pre-feature hosts emit it only for accepted retracts), never fails. The bundle keeps enumerating the known values for codegen and docs. */
export type RetractOutcome = "accepted" | "declined" | (string & {});

/** Whether this host can enforce the sandbox (ADR 50064-startup-report-sandbox-availability D2/D4). A property of the host process, probed once at construction and identical for every session and every connection it serves — never requested, granted, or negotiated, which is why it is not a capability. Open: a future enforcement posture arrives as a new value, read by older clients as unknown. */
export type SandboxAvailability = "available" | "unavailable" | (string & {});

/** Why sandbox enforcement is impossible on this host. Sent only when [`SandboxAvailability`] is `unavailable`; a client MUST NOT infer a reason when none is sent. Open: a future reason arrives as a new value, read by older clients as unknown. */
export type SandboxUnavailableReason = "userNamespacesDenied" | "bubblewrapUnavailable" | (string & {});
## 5. Muse, API keys, and the terminal

The repository records Muse Code 1.4.11, headless exec, stdio MSP, API-key precedence via META_API_KEY, and an observed structured rejection with a bogus key. It does **not** establish successful real-key use or serve environment authentication. Preserve that distinction. Do not invent an endpoint, npm SDK, model capability, or context limit. Discover model availability through the pinned host's catalog and expose a model picker; treat the locally observed model IDs as observations, not contractual availability. [local: plan/BRIEF.md; plan/reference/muse-cli-help.txt]

Concrete key flow: the native settings command accepts the owner's key, stores it in macOS Keychain or Windows Credential Manager, and returns only a credential identifier. Apple documents Keychain as secure credential storage. The key field is transient UI state, cleared immediately and excluded from analytics and state persistence. [verified: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/keychain-services] **[assumption: credential flow]**

For direct Meta requests, the core retrieves the key and sends it through a dedicated inherited credential pipe to the model worker; the adapter supplies the provider's documented authorization scheme after verification. For `muse exec --api-key-stdin`, a separate one-shot child receives it through stdin. For `muse serve --provider meta`, launch with an explicitly constructed environment containing META_API_KEY; its stdin must remain exclusively MSP. Never use argv, a checked-in dotenv file, terminal export commands, or consumer login. On key rotation kill the old host and start a new one. [local: plan/reference/muse-cli-help.txt; plan/BRIEF.md] **[assumption: serve path pending verification]**

Release gate: prove a real-key serve turn is billed to the supplied key, bogus-key failure remains structured, and logged-in consumer credentials cannot become a silent fallback. Also test whether Muse passes META_API_KEY into tool subprocesses; an environment key is exposed to its host and may propagate. Require a scrubbed tool environment or supported secret-isolating adapter before enabling shell tools with real credentials. A protocol wrapper alone cannot enforce that inside a closed child binary. If serve fails auth verification, ship the documented exec key-stdin path while keeping the MSP adapter behind a feature flag. **[assumption]**

Use MSP for the main builder interface: progress, files changed, preview, test output, and approvals. The local schema distinguishes acknowledging presentation of an approval from actually deciding it, and exposes typed auth/quota errors. Follow those semantics; never turn a displayed approval into consent. [local: plan/reference/msp/msp.d.ts: PresentationReceipt, SessionStartParams, QuotaDetail, TurnErrorKind]

Use **xterm.js** only for an advanced terminal drawer backed by a real PTY/ConPTY adapter in Rust. Native terminal widgets would require two integration paths; an MSP userShell request is a command operation, not proof of an interactive PTY. Initially the terminal is a user-started workspace shell; Muse runs headlessly through MSP. Disable automatic clipboard/escape integrations and URL opening, bound scrollback, and keep terminal bytes off analytics. xterm's security guidance explicitly treats terminal access and privileges as application responsibilities. [verified: https://xtermjs.org/docs/guides/security/] [local: plan/reference/msp/msp.d.ts: SessionUserShellParams] **[assumption: terminal design]**

